   "source": "rag-fundamentals.md",
   "excerpt": "Retrieval-augmented generation combines an information retrieval step with\na language model generation step. When a query arrives, the system first\nretrieves relevant document chunks — typically using vector similarity\nsearch over embeddings, keyword search such as BM25, or a hybrid of both —\nand then passes those chunks to a language model as context so the model\ncan synthesize an answer grounded in the retrieved material.",
   "confidence": 0.9
  },
  {
   "claim": "A plain database lookup returns stored records that precisely match a structured query. It does not synthesize information; it simply retrieves the data deterministically by key, index, or predicate.",
   "source": "rag-fundamentals.md",
   "excerpt": "A plain database lookup, by contrast, returns stored records that exactly\nmatch a structured query. It performs no synthesis: the result is the data\nitself, retrieved deterministically by key, index, or predicate. A database\nlookup cannot answer questions whose phrasing does not match the stored\nstructure, and it cannot combine information from several records into a\nnew prose answer.",
   "confidence": 0.9
  },
  {
   "claim": "RAG can handle natural-language queries, tolerate paraphrasing, and produce synthesized prose. However, its answers are probabilistic and may contain errors even with good retrieval.",
   "source": "rag-fundamentals.md",
   "excerpt": "Key differences: RAG handles fuzzy natural-language queries, tolerates\nparaphrasing, and produces synthesized prose, but its answers are\nprobabilistic and can contain errors even with good retrieval.",
